					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/doktori" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1169549); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1169549&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349698" alt="Doktori" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1169549)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/doktori" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1169549); return false;" >Doktori</a></span> <span class="odate time_1329655234 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Feb 2012 12:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1377440">
									

<p>The biggest problem with the neck is how close the major structures are to one another. A little off, and you're putting a hole in the carotid and the person is bleeding like a stuck pig.</p>
<p>The antecubital (elbow) vein is still the best choice from a medical point of view, but it's not as sexy as stabbing someone in the neck : ) The meds we have nowadays for anesthesia knock you out in 2-3 seconds, and I assume the Foundation's are better.</p>
<p>Having said all that, I still like the story. It's just the doctor in me bubbling up.</p>

							</div>

								
					
