														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1377750">
									

<p><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/incident-239-b-clef-kondraki">This</a> is what you're talking about, right? Regarding recognizability, you'll notice that how 732 sounds there isn't remotely like what 732 sounds like here. I'd like to know where the impression it speaks only/mostly in 1337/lolspeak is coming from.</p>
<p>Is this suggestion easier to work with? Get rid of the 1337 in the speak. Slightly alter the speech to sound a little more like it was lifted out of a story. (think &quot;native language of fanfic, struggles with normal-person talk&quot;). For fluid writing style, how about constantly shifting styles throughout the &quot;conversation&quot;, from romance (&quot;Margaret you sound pretty&quot; to &quot;Margaret. What a pretty name, he mused to himself&quot;) to hardboiled (&quot;They call this site 19&quot; already kind of sounds like it) to action (&quot;your in danger do you want me to get you out?&quot; to something more melodramatic).</p>
<p>It would be nice to have more hints that this might not be 732 (unusual activity from other textual SCPs, for instance); the additional evidence you postulated is kind of tenuous. Being aware of increased activity by 732 does not mean you can tell that it's messed with your email. (How can they tell it's increasing, anyway? For all they know, they could have missed corrupted records in earlier surveys.)</p>

							</div>

								
					
