														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1476460">
									

<p>Is this a response, or just a general commentary on the thread?</p>
<p>If the former, I would like to point out that Death of the Author does not cover disconnects between the text and things based on the text; to return to the 963 example, whatever the author's intention in writing the SCP, it is clearly laid out in the text that it overwrites people with one Dr. Jack Bright, and not indicated at all that it turns people into monkeys.</p>
<p>Hence, any story depicting 963 turning people into monkeys is inconsistent with the original SCP. A story that depicts the wearer of 963 as a monkey, and makes no implications about this condition, is legal, but may mislead those unfamiliar with the original SCP.</p>
<p>And any story having it do things unrelated to turning people (or monkeys) into Jack Bright is also inconsistent, unless there is a plausible event X which changes the properties of 963, or convincing reason Y these properties didn't show up previously, or dead giveaway Z that shows we are not actually dealing with 963. Such things must be accounted for in-story, and not out of it as commentary.</p>

							</div>

								
					
