					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1173523&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349698" alt="Eskobar" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1173523)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;" >Eskobar</a></span> <span class="odate time_1339362046 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Jun 2012 21:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1476502">
									

<p>I might point out that you have no way of proving, author or not, that SCP-732 doesn't or can't alter its manner of communication. Nor can you prove that it does or does not have motives beyond that of general carnage, or that it is or is not sentient, or that SCP-732 even <em>is</em> the fan-fic plague (what if the fan-fic plague altered another SCP's record to make it look like a fan-fic plague?) That's the nature of corrupted documents; large portions of the article have been changed, with no way of proving what it said beforehand. It's like redaction, except without knowing anything substantive about the sanity or motives of the redactor.</p>
<p>Look, I like your article, and part of why I like it is because of the ambiguously powerful nature of an entity that can corrupt its own containment. But something that goes along with ambiguity is the corrolary difference in opinion that comes along with what the entity is or can do. I can believe SCP-732 is a more broadly powerful entity with singularly unusual abilities and completely inscrutable motives. You, meanwhile, can simply believe the entity in the Tale isn't SCP-732; once again, if we're going to judge simply by what is written on the page, nothing here says SCP-732 is involved with the primary plot of the story in any way. Headcanon is what it is, and we're just going to have to disagree.</p>

							</div>

								
					
